"""Plain-language appendix for the executed history and speaker-label pipeline."""
from html import escape as e

def render(data,details,table,link,first_table=8):
    body='<div id="implementation-details"><ul class="brief"><li>Earlier calls receive the whole transcript so far and all valid earlier note snapshots.</li><li>Diarization receives all audio so far; timestamp filtering selects new ASR words for the saved transcript.</li><li>Reference answers use AMI participant labels, connected to predicted speaker labels through a saved time-overlap correspondence.</li><li>Saved outputs show stable anonymous channels on the checked regions, while some annotation-based labels change between prefixes.</li></ul><nav class="jump-links" aria-label="Implementation appendix sections">'+link('#history-construction','Transcript and notes')+link('#new-word-selection','New words and speaker labels')+link('#reference-speakers','Reference speaker identities')+link('#speaker-consistency-audit','Consistency audit')+'</nav></div>'
    history='<p>A <strong>transcript prefix</strong> is all transcript available before a cutoff; a <strong>chunk</strong> is the newly arrived segment. A <strong>note snapshot</strong> is one completed summary or structured-note JSON output. Each earlier call contains a system message and one user message with separate <code>past_transcript</code> and <code>previous_results</code> fields.</p>'
    history+='<pre class="prompt">'+e('Per-meeting call layout (schematic):\n\nUpdate 1: [system] [transcript: chunk 1] [earlier notes: none]\n          → snapshot 1\nUpdate 2: [system] [transcript: chunks 1 + 2] [snapshot 1]\n          → snapshot 2\nUpdate 3: [system] [transcript: chunks 1 + 2 + 3] [snapshots 1 + 2]\n          → snapshot 3\n\nQuestion: [answering system] [full available transcript]\n          [all valid snapshots before the question] [question]\n          → answer')+'</pre>'
    history+='<p>Earlier snapshots are quoted as data inside the user message. The caller rebuilds this pair of messages for every call; it does not append each new chunk after the preceding notes as a continued sequence of user and model messages. vLLM prefix caching is enabled as a computation optimization.</p><p>Each structured snapshot can contain up to twelve notes. The caller appends a valid snapshot to the previous snapshots, so the accumulated input can exceed twelve entries. Outputs are checked for format and available source IDs; repeated facts are retained. Only the final summary or note JSON is reused. Earlier native reasoning text contributes to the token count but is absent from subsequent prompts.</p>'
    rows=[]
    for r in data['note_repetition']:
        rows.append([r['model'],str(r['valid_note_entries']),str(r['entries_repeated_from_an_earlier_snapshot']),str(r['example_snapshots']),str(r['example_entries']),str(r['example_distinct_normalized_facts'])])
    history+=table(first_table,'Structured-note repetition in the ground-truth runs; the example is the EN2002b note-taking question.', ['Reader','All valid entries','Repeated from earlier snapshots','Example snapshots','Example entries','Example distinct fact texts'],rows)
    history+='<p>The repetition check normalizes case and punctuation, then compares each fact text with earlier valid snapshots from the same meeting. It counts exact-text recurrence; paraphrased repetition and factual correctness require separate inspection. The Thinking example contains seven snapshots, seventy entries and fourteen distinct normalized fact texts. Its request input grows from 5,447 reactive tokens to 8,797 structured-note tokens. Instruct has ten snapshots, ninety-nine entries and twelve distinct fact texts; its inputs grow from 5,445 to 11,336 tokens.</p><p>The context-fit function can drop the oldest snapshots to reserve room for output and logs their IDs. No calls omitted snapshots in these two ground-truth runs. '+link('../research/answering-report/implementation-audit.json','Verified counts and source excerpts')+'</p><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> these runs measure full-prefix reading with accumulated snapshots. Updated current notes or an interleaved stream of incremental additions are proposed alternatives; they have not been evaluated here.</p>'
    history+='<p>The ground-truth Thinking mean also needs a per-question reading: structured notes reduce request-time thinking on two of the six questions and increase it on four. The note-taking question falls from 5,000 to 2,156 tokens and the folder question from 2,270 to 1,169. Excluding the note-taking question, the remaining five use 8,736 tokens with structured notes versus 8,669 reactively. The aggregate 20.3% reduction is concentrated in particular cases.</p>'
    body+=details('How the executed S2 calls combine transcripts and saved notes',history,id='history-construction')
    words='<p>Each update makes a new diarization call using the <strong>entire audio prefix from the meeting start</strong>. ASR processes the new chunk with up to two seconds of preceding audio. Word timestamps connect the ASR output to the speaker activity predicted for that prefix.</p>'
    words+=table(first_table+1,'Actual audio spans for the first two EN2002b updates, in seconds.', ['Update','New chunk','Diarization input','ASR input','New-word selection'],[
        ['First','0–29.5','0–29.5','0–29.5','Word midpoint at or after 0 s'],
        ['Second','29.5–60.5','0–60.5','27.5–60.5','Discard words whose midpoint is before 29.5 s']])
    words+='<p>The annotation function first discards ASR words whose original midpoint is before the new chunk start, with a 0.0001 s numerical tolerance. It clips word ends to the chunk end and rejects invalid intervals. Words crossing the left boundary are assigned using their midpoint and can retain a start time just before the boundary. ASR alignment errors can still produce repeated or omitted boundary words.</p><p>For each retained word, the code selects the speaker with the greatest predicted activity overlap around its timestamp, using a 0.04 s margin. If there is no overlap, the label is <code>unknown</code>. If the two leading overlap amounts differ by less than 0.05 s and both are positive, the winning label is still assigned and an <code>overlap_ambiguous</code> flag is recorded. Consecutive words are then grouped into speaker-attributed segments. Only these new segments are appended; older saved segments remain in place.</p>'
    words+='<p><strong>Illustrative arrival order:</strong> Alice speaks at 5 s and Bob at 12 s in a first 0–30 s chunk. In the new 30–60 s chunk, Bob speaks at 35 s before Alice at 45 s. A second call on 0–60 s still encounters Alice at 5 s before Bob at 12 s, so it can retain Alice as Speaker0 and Bob as Speaker1. A call on only 30–60 s would encounter them in the opposite order. Our caller uses the full prefix. Sortformer assigns channels following speaker arrival order, as described in the '+link('https://huggingface.co/nvidia/diar_streaming_sortformer_4spk-v2.1','NVIDIA model card')+'.</p>'
    body+=details('How full-prefix diarization annotates only the newly saved words',words,id='new-word-selection')
    refs='<p>AMI participant labels A–D identify the annotated participants. Predicted labels Speaker0–Speaker3 identify diarizer output channels. Their correspondence is estimated from the time overlap between predicted speaker activity and AMI annotated utterances within the available prefix. The code chooses a one-to-one assignment maximizing total overlap; any assigned pair with positive overlap is accepted, without an additional strong-confidence threshold.</p>'
    mapping=data['note_question_example']['predicted_to_AMI']
    refs+=table(first_table+2,'Saved correspondence at the EN2002b note-taking request, 303.62 s.', ['Predicted speaker channel','AMI participant label'],[[e(k),e(v)] for k,v in sorted(mapping.items())])
    refs+='<p>The reference says <strong>“D volunteered to take the notes.”</strong> An automatic-condition answer saying <strong>“Speaker1 volunteered”</strong> identifies the corresponding participant under this saved mapping. A–D are not assumed to match the numerical order of the predicted channels.</p><ul><li><strong>Automatic speaker condition:</strong> the reader sees predicted labels; the correspondence with AMI is used during source-based answer review.</li><li><strong>Annotation-mapped condition:</strong> the correspondence also converts predicted labels to A–D in the transcript supplied to the reader; requester identity is supplied from the annotations.</li><li><strong>Reference answers:</strong> the saved references remain in A–D labels. They were not automatically rewritten into Speaker0–Speaker3 answers. Codex source review applies the saved correspondence and checks the requested facts; literal label equality is insufficient.</li></ul><p>Reference answers and scoring labels are excluded from reader inputs. Ground-truth speaker annotations enter the reader input through the explicitly labeled annotation-mapped diagnostic. In automatic runs, requester matching uses the question voice and previously heard predicted speaker speech.</p><p>Best-overlap matching can be uncertain when a channel mixes participants or contains little speech. The audit below also shows that the A–D correspondence can change as the prefix grows, while earlier notes retain previous labels. These limits qualify identity-dependent judgments and the annotation-mapped amortized comparisons.</p>'
    body+=details('How reference speaker identities are matched to predicted labels',refs,id='reference-speakers')
    audit=data['speaker_consistency']
    checks='<p>A read-only audit compares 61 saved diarization outputs from five meetings: fifty consecutive shared-chunk pairs and six request branches, for 56 prefix comparisons. Parakeet and Whisper share the same diarization outputs. Within the common past audio, the audit compares regions where each run predicts exactly one active speaker and finds the speaker-channel permutation with greatest matched duration.</p><p><strong>Observed:</strong> all checked single-speaker regions preserve the original channel identity, and none of the 56 comparisons gains more than 0.05 s by changing the permutation. This holds with the whole common interval and with the last 1.04 s of the older prefix excluded. Synthetic checks verify detection of a swap, a merge with no perfect permutation, and a zero-overlap case. This measures cross-prefix consistency; a stable channel can still mix people, and simultaneous-speech errors need separate assessment.</p>'
    changes=audit['alias_changes']
    checks+=table(first_table+3,'Annotation-label changes across consecutive note-prefix inputs.', ['Type of change','Parakeet segments','Whisper segments'],[
        ['Existing A–D label changed to another A–D label',str(changes['parakeet']['identity_reassigned']),str(changes['whisper']['identity_reassigned'])],
        ['Existing A–D label became an unmapped channel label',str(changes['parakeet']['name_removed']),str(changes['whisper']['name_removed'])],
        ['Previously unmapped channel received its first A–D label',str(changes['parakeet']['first_name_assignment']),str(changes['whisper']['first_name_assignment'])]])
    checks+='<p>These are segment-label change events across updates, so the same segment can be counted more than once. First assignments can be appropriate resolution of an earlier unknown identity. The confirmed inconsistency is that the current mapping is reapplied to the accumulated transcript while earlier saved notes are not explicitly updated or invalidated.</p><p>For example, an earlier EN2003a segment labeled C becomes B when the prefix reaches 66 s. No additional label change was observed between each request input and its immediately preceding shared-prefix input. Effects on saved-note claims and final answers remain unmeasured.</p><p><strong>Proposed repairs:</strong> maintain stable speaker IDs separately from annotation labels, verify cross-prefix matches with sufficient common speech, retain uncertainty for weak matches, and explicitly revise notes affected by identity changes. A controlled identity repair should hold the note-accumulation policy fixed so its effects can be measured separately from deduplication. Ground-truth transcript and S1 results are independent of this label-mapping issue.</p><p>The checks use zero GPUs, with no GPU roles, shared services or GPU dependencies; maximum zero active GPUs. Experiment inputs, outputs and scores are preserved. '+link('../research/answering-report/speaker-prefix-comparisons.json','All prefix comparisons')+' · '+link('../research/answering-report/speaker-annotation-changes.json','All annotation-label change events')+'</p>'
    body+=details('What the saved-prefix identity audit establishes and what remains to fix',checks,id='speaker-consistency-audit')
    excerpts='<p>These excerpts are copied from the executed caller and builder sources. They show the input fields, selection rules and mapping logic discussed above.</p>'
    for r in data['source_excerpts']:
        excerpts+='<h3>'+e(r['label'])+'</h3><p class="small" style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">'+e(r['file'])+' · lines '+str(r['line_start'])+'–'+str(r['line_end'])+'</p><pre class="prompt">'+e(r['code'])+'</pre>'
    body+=details('Source excerpts for history construction and speaker handling',excerpts,id='implementation-source')
    return body
